---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteNav from '../components/SiteNav.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import Doc from '../components/Doc.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import { categories } from '../components/data';

/**
 * About. Written in the site's voice, and careful about one thing: it makes no
 * claim about who "we" are beyond what is true. No headcount, no founding
 * date, no location, no invented bylines. The counts in the fact strip are
 * computed from the real shortlists so they cannot drift out of date.
 */
const facts = [
  { n: String(categories.length), label: 'categories' },
  { n: String(categories.reduce((t, c) => t + c.picks.length, 0)), label: 'picks in total' },
  { n: String(categories.filter((c) => c.comparison).length), label: 'head-to-heads' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="About OSI Affiliate — why this site is short"
  description="OSI Affiliate is a curated software directory for small businesses and solopreneurs: a handful of real picks per category instead of an open marketplace, with the business model stated plainly."
>
  <SiteNav />
  <main>
    <PageHead
      title="Why this site is short"
      lede="OSI Affiliate is a software directory built on the opposite bet from the big ones. They win by listing everything. We are trying to be useful by listing almost nothing."
    >
      <ul class="facts">
        {
          facts.map((f) => (
            <li>
              <span class="facts-n">{f.n}</span>
              <span class="facts-l">{f.label}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </PageHead>

    <Doc>
      <section>
        <h2>Who it is for</h2>
        <p class="doc-lead">
          Someone running a small business who needs to pick a CRM, an email platform, a website
          builder, a project tool or an SEO tool — this month, once — and get back to work.
        </p>
        <p>
          That includes solopreneurs buying their first proper tool, owners replacing something
          that has stopped fitting, and in-house marketers who have been handed the decision and
          a browser full of tabs. It is not built for procurement teams running a formal
          evaluation, and it is not built for people who enjoy comparing software.
        </p>
        <p>
          The assumption behind every page is that you want a defensible answer and a reason,
          not a filterable database.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What is actually different here</h2>
        <p>
          Search “best CRM” and you land on a marketplace with over a thousand listings under
          that one category. It is an impressive asset and a terrible answer to the question you
          asked. Sorting it requires you to already know what matters, which is the thing you
          came to find out.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>Two to four picks per category.</b> The shortlist is the product. If a tool no
            longer earns its slot it comes off, rather than being kept for the extra link.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Every pick carries a reason to skip it.</b> Written by us, in the entry, not
            buried in a pros-and-cons vote count. A recommendation with no stated limit is an
            advert.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>No ratings, no review counts, no testimonials.</b> This site is new and has none
            that are real, so it has none at all.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>The business model is on the page.</b> Not in a footer line in grey six-point
            type — at the top of every page that carries a vendor link.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How this site makes money</h2>
        <p>
          Affiliate commission. If you follow a link from here and sign up with a vendor, they
          may pay us a share of what you pay them, at no extra cost to you. That is the same
          mechanism the large directories use, and on its own it is fine. What is not fine is
          the arrangement we refuse: nobody can pay to be on a shortlist here, pay to sit higher
          on one, or pay to soften what an entry says.
        </p>
        <p>
          The full version, including exactly where the site stands today on which links earn
          anything, is on the <a href="/disclosure">disclosure page</a>, and the rules that
          decide the picks are on <a href="/how-we-pick">how we pick</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Where the site is today</h2>
        <p>
          New, and saying so. {categories.length} categories are live, with {
            categories.reduce((t, c) => t + c.picks.length, 0)
          } picks between them and {categories.filter((c) => c.comparison).length} head-to-head
          comparisons where the “X or Y” question is common enough to deserve its own page.
        </p>
        <p>
          The picks come from research rather than from running every tool in a live business,
          and the entries say so where it matters. Categories and comparisons get added when
          real reader demand shows up, not to fill out a sitemap — so if a comparison you want
          is missing, asking for it is the fastest way to make it exist.
        </p>
        <div class="doc-inset">
          <p>
            One note on voice: “we” here means whoever wrote and checked the page you are
            reading. Entries carry the site’s name rather than personal bylines, because the
            judgement being offered is the site’s and the site is what you should hold to it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Tell us what we are missing</h2>
        <p>
          The two most useful messages we get are “this pricing line is out of date” and “you
          have left the obvious tool off this list”. Both change the site.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/contact" class="pill pill-line">Get in touch</a>
        </p>
      </section>
    </Doc>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Three counted facts, taken from the real shortlists. Set at heading scale
     but not at hero scale — they are context, not the point of the page. */
  .facts {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1px;
    margin: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.25rem) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    background: var(--line);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    width: fit-content;
  }

  .facts li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    background: var(--color-brand-bg);
    padding: 0.75rem 1.15rem;
  }

  .facts-n {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-size: 1.45rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--color-brand-primary);
  }

  .facts-l {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-brand-text) 66%, transparent);
  }
</style>
